1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fotografiet föreställer Holm kyrka vid Sjöö slott i Uppland som Andreas var med om att bygga under sin tid som kyrkoherde där.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Occupation:'''  Rector of congregation ("kyrkoherde" in Swedish), head priest, head vicar.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Picture:'''  The Family Coat of Arms of the Rector Andreas Hackzelius in 1663.  It may have been in use already long before that year by Andreas and/or his ancestors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Andreas Laurenitt Hackzelius''' (born in 1630 in Hacksta, Uppland, Sweden) was a Swedish clergyman.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas was born to Lars Eriksson (1605-1674) and Karin Matsdotter (1610-1682) in midst of the 30 Years' War (1618–1648). When Andreas turned 18, the war ended. This freed Andreas – instead of soldiering – to pursue a career close
2 CONC r to his heart. Andreas applied and was accepted to Uppsala University to study theology and languages. Andreas became a clergyman.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his career choice Andreas detached himself as well as all his future offspring from the family's long time tradition of farming and operating a plantation, which his father Lars, grandfather Erik and great-grandfather Bengt h
2 CONC ad represented in Uppland, Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life as a minister'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Andreas' inauguration to the Lutheran ministry, the churchly commissions of Andreas came to include - among other things - the heading of congregations of the parishes of Kulla and Holm in the Lagunda Hundred in Uppland, Swed
2 CONC en.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas Hackzelius became the first rector – head of a congregation – to oversee the newly constructed Church of Holm, erected in 1678 in the Parish of Holm, between Lårstaviken and Gorran in Mälaren, 17 km north of Bålsta, in the
2 CONC  municipality of Enköping in Uppland, Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT The new church was closely connected with the nearby Sjöö Castle (modern spelling: Sjö), which was built at the same time with the church. The construction work of the castle lasted from 1669 until 1679. The castle was owned by a 
2 CONC patron (Latin: patrōnus) of the church, the Marshal of the Realm of Sweden Johan Gabriel Stenbock (1640–1705), one of the most powerful people in Sweden in the 17th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 17th century, the patronage of the Church of Holm consisted of high ranking statesmen, clergymen, military officers as well as royal and other high noble friends and family members of Stenbock. In a 17th century letter rema
2 CONC ining, the Queen of Sweden Christina (1626–1689) compliments and thanks Rector Andreas Hackzelius for a church service and sermon given by him.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 28, 1671, Hackzelius married Brita Mårtensdotter Hörling (1649–1710) in Stockholm, Sweden. The couple had a son, Mårten Hacksell (1674–1725), who became a commander and a senior enforcement officer for the Swedish crown
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Andreas Hackzelius died on July 7, 1681, in Uppland, Sweden. Among his best known descendants is the WWII-era Prime Minister of Finland Antti Hackzell (1881–1946).
2 CONT
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var kyrkoherde i Holms sn I Uppland. Han kallade sig Hackzelius efter sin hemsocken. De fick 6 barn.
2 CONT
