1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Tornio - Torneå Vihityt Kuulutettu / Vihitty 	  	18.1.1787 Mies 	Kylä / Talo 	Torneå stad 	 
2 CONT Nimi 	Hr: vice (Befallningsm:) 	Zacharias 	  	Hackzell Vaimo 	Kylä / Talo 	Torneå stad 	 Nimi 	M:elle 	Agatha 	  	Pipping  https://hiski.genealogia.fi/hiski?fi+t8067965
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Zacharias Hackzell''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Zacharias was born in March 1, 1751, in Tornio, Lapland, Finland.  He died in August 20, 1804, in Finland.   Zacharias worked as the chief of police (Swedish: kronolänsman) for the Swedish crown in Tornio, Finland.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Childhood'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Zacharias was a one of the five children born to Anders Mårtensson Hackzell (1705-1757) and Anna Catharina Plantin (1663-1741), of which three were sons (one died at birth) and two were daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT The father of Zacharias, Anders Hackzell, was a chief enforcement officer (Swedish: kronofogde), cartographer and mapper (Swedish: lantmätare) for the Swedish crown. Two weeks before the sixth birthday of Zacharias, his father had
2 CONC  died in February 13, 1757.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Marriage and career'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Zacharias married twice. The first wife of Zacharias, Anna Margareta Grape, died at the age of 29 in 1785. Zacharias later married to Agata Pipping (1766-1833), the daughter of the mayor of Tornio, Peter Pipping. |
2 CONT
2 CONT Following in the footsteps of both his father Anders Hackzell and grandfather Mårten Hackzell, Zacharias too chose to work for the Swedish crown. Zacharias became the chief of police (Swedish: kronolänsman) for the Swedish crown i
2 CONC n Tornio, at the border of the modern-day countries of Sweden and Finland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time, Finland was still a part of the Realm of Sweden (a.k.a. Sweden-Finland). Five years after the death of Zacharias in 1809, Finland became an autonomous Grand Duchy of Russia (1809-1917).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref.:
2 CONT *[http://runeberg.org/amhistupp/5/0169.html  	Historiska upplysningar om Religiösa rörelserna i Finland i äldre och nyare tider ] del 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''An epitome of the Bure family kinship'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hackzell family name derives from the Hacksta family estate, located in Hacksta, Uppland in Sweden. Through Mårten Hackzell, the only child of the Uppland clergyman Andreas Hackzelius, and through Mårten's children, including 
2 CONC Anders Hackzell - the father of Zacharias Hackzell -, the Hackzell family spread to Norrland and Finland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Among the best-known 20th century members of the Hackzell family is the World-War-II-period Prime Minister of Finland Antti Hackzell (1881–1946).
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Mårten and his son Anders married to women from the culturally important and well-documented Scandinavian Bure kinship (Swedish: Bureätten) in Norrland, making Zacharias Hackzell - son of Anders - an epitome of the Bure kinsh
2 CONC ip genotype in the 18th century, for this and the following reasons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Both the father and mother of Zacharias descended from separate children of the married couple of Anders Persson Grubb (1468-1543) and Mariet Margareta Jakobsdotter Bure (1473-1529).
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders Persson Grubb and his wife Mariet were both Bure kinship members and second cousins to each other, both descending from Olof "Gamle Olof" Hersesson Bure (1380-1460).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Head of Congregation Nicolaus Olai Plantin (1629-1685), a maternal great-grandfather of Zacharias, was also a Bure kinship descendant through both of his parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Viking Age Bure lineages'''
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Viking Age, spanning from the 8th century to the late 11th century, influential Bure family members were born in the important trading centers of Sigtuna and Birka in Uppland, a part of the modern-day central Sweden. Ba
2 CONC sed on primary sources,
2 CONT
2 CONT Uppland's Bure kinship members included the Viking Prince ("herse") Herse Bure (born in c. 940); his son the Viking Chieftain ("stor hövding", "storman") Tord I Byr (born in 975 in Byr, Sko, Sigtuna, Uppland); and his son King ("h
2 CONC erse, kung") Gunnar Haursi (Herse / Bure), a Viking merchant (born in 1010 Skokloster, Sko, Sigtuna, Uppland / died in Birka, Björkö, Mälaren, Uppland).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to old written records and medieval evidence such as the rhimestone Burestenen, the Bure kinship also inhabited during the late Viking Age some coastal areas in what today is the Province of Medelpad, by the Gulf of Both
2 CONC nia, in the south-central part of Norrland, north of the historical province of Uppland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The areas with a significant Bure kinship presence there included the community named 'Bure' in the township of Selånger in the modern-day area of Sundsvall, where Thord Jerker Bure (born in c. 1035, Birka, Uppland), a son of King
2 CONC  Gunnar Haursi, died in the return of the 11th century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thord's son Erik Thordsson Bure was born in Bure in 1060 and Erik's son Herser Eriksson Bure in 1085. Herser's son, Justice of the Peace ("landsdomare") Fale Hin Gamle Bure, was also born in the modern-day area of Sundsvall, in By
2 CONC restad (modern-day "Birsta") in the township of Skön ("Sköns socken") in 1115. He died in May 16, 1161, in Fale Bro, Uppsala, Uppland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The following seven generations of the direct male lineage descendants, the offspring of Fale Hin Gamle Bure, were also all born in Byrestad, in the area of the modern-day City of Sundsvall. The town was chartered in 1621, and the
2 CONC  first urban plan for Sundsvall is believed to have been created by Olof Bure in 1642, less likely in 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the latter half of the 14th century onward, the center of gravity of the recorded Bure kinship began shifting northbound, to the area today known as the Province of Västerbotten in northern Sweden. In the late Middle Ages, th
2 CONC e epicenter of the Bure kinship there was located in the area of Skellefteå (historically known as "Heletti" in Finnish), and particularly in the community of Bureå there.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bure family lineages can be confirmed from various sources and by much evidence fairly reliably from the end of the 15th century onward, although already long before the modern critical approach the family lineages were extend
2 CONC ed and recorded also all the way back to the Viking Age. From the late 12th century on the Bure family is well documented.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bure kinship genealogical records'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest known extensive Bure family genealogy was written in the beginning of the 17th century by Johannes Bureus, in his manuscript Om Bura namn och ätt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In his study, Bureus included all the family ancestors and descendants, whether male or female and regardless of what social standing or legitimacy each family member might have represented, thus making it possible for many modern
2 CONC -day families to trace their ancestry back to the Bure kinship. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The manuscript Om Bura namn och ätt is located at Riksarkivet ("State Archives"), and also at the Uppsala University Library (numbers X36 and X37).
2 CONT
2 CONT The history of the Bure kinship is complemented by the studies of Nils Burman (1705–1750), who wrote about the Bure family history until the middle of the 18th century. Some Bure family lines and individuals have acquired the name
2 CONC s Bure, Burman and Burensköld. Some Bure family lines - as well as individuals from other family lines - are noble.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cultural importance of the Bure family genealogy in Sweden is demonstrated e.g. by the novel The Knight Templar trilogy by Jan Guillou, which in part compares with the early Bure family ancestry. The trilogy features fictional
2 CONC  events fitted upon the framework of the Bure kinship.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Photo:   The current profile picture on this page shows Burestenen, a memorial runestone honoring a deceased Bure family member in c. 1020–1050 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT This runestone is located a short distance south from the City of Sundsvall, in the Swedish province of Medelpad by the Gulf of Bothnia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Old Norse text on the stone translates into English as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bergsven and Sigfast and Fride raised this stone in memory of Bure, their father. Fartägn carved it."
